Subject: [overlay 0047] gl_dlight_loop — a per-light early-out in the dynamic-light loop,
         switchable live, default OFF

Measured on the test Vision Pro (2026-09-06, VR Render Quality 1.75 => 3360x3192 per eye,
120 Hz layer). The engine's GPU time per published frame, `VRGPU eng_ms` p50/p95:

    baseline                      ~15 / 17-22 ms
    cl_shadowlights 0              4.9 / 7.3 ms
    gl_per_pixel_lighting 0        3.8 / 6.7 ms
    vr_sharpen 0 (CAS off)         no change

So roughly two thirds of the engine's GPU time is the per-pixel dynamic-light shading, and
this is the loop that does it. What that loop does not do is reject a light before shading
it. The distance test is arithmetic, not a branch:

    float len = max(radius - dist - DLIGHT_CUTOFF, 0.0) / radius;

`len` is zero for every pixel outside the light's radius, and the body still runs in full for
those pixels: a length(), two dot products, the cone math, the colour multiply. A light four
thousand units away costs exactly what a light touching the surface costs.

That would be a small waste if the shipped light set were the lights that actually reach the
pixel. It is not. Under the default `gl_dlight_batchkey 0` a batch's light set is the UNION
over every face that shares texture and state (tess.c ~925), so a batch routinely carries a
double-digit light count while any one pixel is inside the radius of two or three of them.
base1 defines 37 shadow lights; the pose this patch was verified at ships 21 of them into the
batch UBOs (`DRAWDIAG ... ubits=0000001f0000ffff`). At 10.7 MP per eye, twice per frame, the
lights the pixel is nowhere near are most of the bill.

`gl_dlight_loop` selects how the loop rejects:

  0  upstream, byte for byte. THE DEFAULT, and it stays the default until the headset
     A/B says otherwise — the charter's rule is measure before optimizing, and the Mac cannot
     measure this (see below).
  1  after `dist` is computed: `if (dist >= dlights[i].radius) continue;`
     Exactly the condition under which `len` is 0 (`radius` in the shader is the light's
     radius PLUS DLIGHT_CUTOFF, so `radius - dist - CUTOFF > 0` reduces to
     `dist < dlights[i].radius`). Skips the lambert, the cone and the colour multiply.
  2  before the `length()`: `if (dot(d,d) >= r*r) continue;`. Same condition, and it also
     skips the square root.
  3  a control that is WRONG ON PURPOSE — it rejects at half the radius. 1 and 2 cannot
     change a pixel by construction, so "the picture did not change" is equally consistent
     with "the early-out is exact" and with "the uniform never reached the shader and mode 0
     ran the whole time". Mode 3 is unmistakable on screen, so switching to it and back is a
     one-command proof that the branch is live, in the simulator and in the headset alike.
     Never a shipping value.

PRECISION, because mode 2 squares things and the fragment shader is `precision mediump
float` (shader.c ~line 67). On ANGLE-Metal mediump is half: max 65504. A squared distance
across a map (8192^2 = 6.7e7) blows through that, and so does a large light's radius squared
(1000^2 = 1e6). Two infinities compared against each other would decide the branch by
accident rather than by geometry, so the squared compare in mode 2 is declared `highp`
explicitly. Precision qualifiers are accepted (and ignored) by desktop GLSL 1.30+, so the
same source compiles for the macOS oracle. Modes 0 and 1 are untouched by any of this.

WHY IT IS A UNIFORM AND NOT A #define. The entire point is that it can be toggled in the
headset, mid-session, over the dev console and read `q2vrpace` — an A/B that needs a shader
recompile or a vid_restart is an A/B that will not get run. So the loop is emitted once per mode
with the mode branch OUTSIDE it, selected by `u_dlight_loop`, which rides a former `pad` slot
in the existing Uniforms block: no new buffer, no new upload, and the std140 layout is
unchanged (a `float pad_5` became a `float u_dlight_loop`; its C mirror `GLfloat pad` became
`GLfloat dlight_loop`). It is written in shader_setup_3d alongside u_time and u_modulate.
Mode 0 therefore runs upstream's loop with nothing whatsoever added inside it, and the branch
that selects it is uniform-valued — fully coherent across every quad on any GPU that matters.
The four copies are why MAX_SHADER_CHARS goes from 4096 to 12288; the emitter Q_asserts on
overflow, so that has to lead the change rather than follow it.

VERIFIED, on the Apple Vision Pro simulator, base1, rerelease data, 1920x1824 per eye, one
pinned pose with 21 shadow lights in the batch set, the world PAUSED so the comparison has a
zero noise floor (unpaused, two mode-0 captures already differ on 7.7 % of pixels). Same eye,
across modes, 3502080 pixels, no tolerance — a pixel counts as differing if any channel
differs by one:

    mode 0 vs mode 0 (x4)   0 differing pixels
    mode 0 vs mode 1        0 differing pixels
    mode 0 vs mode 2        0 differing pixels
    mode 1 vs mode 2        0 differing pixels
    mode 0 vs mode 3        2582436 pixels (73.7 %), max abs 129   <- the switch is live
    mode 0 vs ppl off       2724599 pixels (77.8 %), max abs 240   <- the frame is lit

Full method and artifacts: artifacts/vr-r22/VERIFY-dlight-loop.md.

NO PERFORMANCE NUMBER IS CLAIMED HERE. The Mac's GPU renders this scene with eng_ms p95 under
0.4 ms; it cannot resolve the gain, and a number from it would be a fiction. The measurement
happens in the headset, with `gl_dlight_loop 0|1|2` and `q2vrpace`, and the default only
moves after it.

